WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:05.280
At the end of every episode of Journey To
The Microscosmos, we thank you for coming

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:09.960
on this journey with us as we explore the
unseen world that surrounds us.

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:15.180
But have you ever found yourself wanting to
have your own journey and explore the unseen

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:16.680
world that surrounds you?

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:20.880
Well you can start on your own journey with
the Microcosmos Microscope!

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:26.100
We worked with our Master of Microscopes James
Weiss to design what we believe is the perfect

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:29.700
starter microscope for someone who wants to
get into microscopy.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:33.900
In fact, the clip you’re seeing on screen
right now was captured using our microscope.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:38.040
We’ll have a whole video coming out pretty
soon where we talk more about our microscope

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:42.180
and show you what it’s capable of, but for
now, if you’d like to know more or pick

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:45.420
one up for yourself, you&nbsp;
can go to microcosmos.store

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:12.780
If you have been venturing through the microcosmos
for some time now, these oval green shapes

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:14.340
likely seem familiar to you.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:19.260
They are not particularly distinctive, but
they are green and big enough to see

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:20.100
under the microscope.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:25.680
And those two qualities describe a good chunk
of what we find in the microcosmos.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:31.020
These particular little green organisms show
up in the background of other organism’s

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:34.320
lives, providing pops of color among other
debris.

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:39.300
And if you’re trying to identify what this is,
you might turn to some of the obvious candidates.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:42.420
Maybe a euglenoid, or some kind of algae.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:47.460
Something green and rod-shaped and single-celled,
possibly eukaryotic.

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:50.520
And if that’s your guess, we would
not blame you.

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:51.780
But you are wrong.

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:59.340
What you are looking at is not a single-celled
organism, but rather a gathering of them.

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:04.200
Those green bits are consortia of bacteria.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:10.080
We’re going to explain more about what is
actually in that consortia, but before that,

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:12.420
we've got to tell you what a consortium is.

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:17.220
First, not all consortia are green.

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:22.320
Some are more like the colorless squiggle
you see in the middle of your screen, made

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:26.100
up of thin, curved prokaryotes gathered around&nbsp;

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:29.460
a central bacterium that&nbsp;
has flagella at both ends.

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:34.080
And “consortium” is kind of a funny word to
describe bacteria, isn’t it?

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:38.340
It makes them sound like a group of companies
that have come together to sign some fancy

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:39.240
legal document.

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:41.280
Which I suppose in a way they have.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:44.280
There isn't a legal document binding them
together.

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:46.560
There is simply survival.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:51.900
That word “consortium” can mean a lot
of things in the microbial world, in part

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:56.880
because our understanding of what they are
is currently evolving.

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:02.220
But also, there are just a lot of different
contexts in which they seem to appear.

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:09.060
When the term was first introduced to the
microcosmos realm in 1872 by naturalist Johannes

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:16.140
Reinke, the idea was to describe how an organism
can actually be made up of two species that

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:18.900
have somehow found a unified body to live in--

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:22.200
organisms like lichen, made up of algae and
fungi.

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:28.440
But the specific ways that organisms intertwine
their lives can vary, and for our purposes

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:34.140
today, the definition that probably best applies
is that a consortium is a group of bacteria

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:38.640
that are organized and are in permanent contact
with one another.

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:44.160
There are various types of consortia that
scientists know about, including formations

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:46.440
that live in your own mouth.

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:51.840
These consortia can be distinguished from
one another by their color, and their shape, the

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:55.080
number of cells within them, and the shape
of those cells.

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:57.360
And what makes them all special is that these&nbsp;

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:01.080
are relationships formed&nbsp;
entirely between prokaryotes.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:06.780
This sets consortia apart from other ventures
into bacterial symbiosis that we have explored,

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:11.700
which have generally involved bacteria seeking
out more complex eukaryotic partners.

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:16.800
For example, there are the bacteria that live
on the surface of the ciliate kentrophoros

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:22.260
so they can hitch a ride to regions with enough
hydrogen sulfide for them to convert into food.

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:26.340
So this property of consortia&nbsp;
only being relationships&nbsp;

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:30.240
between prokaryotes makes this really fascinating

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:32.100
to scientists for a few reasons.

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:38.400
One is simply that bacteria probably teamed
up with each other before they ever teamed

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:43.800
up with eukaryotess, and understanding how
they formed these relationships may help us

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:47.640
better grasp how they have evolved with the
world around them.

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:50.700
Consortia offer more than just history though.

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:56.520
They also might suggest different futures
for us, providing us with the means to understand

00:04:56.520 --> 00:05:02.280
how we can use bacteria for everything from
farming to cleaning up oil spills.

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:07.440
But identifying and understanding these consortia
is challenging.

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:13.200
James, our master of microscopes, has been
baffled by what looks like a bunch of logs

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:13.860
glued together.

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:18.060
He thought there might be flagellates in there
along with the prokaryotes.

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:21.480
But when he tried separating the individuals,
he couldn't find any.

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:27.000
So for now, like most of the consortia we’ve
been looking at, the mechanics of this group

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:29.460
remain unclear to us.

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:33.480
Perhaps the best studied&nbsp;
consortium is Chlorochromatium&nbsp;

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:37.080
aggregatum, the small green group we watched

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:38.220
in the beginning of this episode.

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:44.340
They are found in lakes and ponds, typically
in areas with low amounts of oxygen and light.

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:51.480
The green bacteria are Chlorobium chlorochromatii,
a green sulfur bacteria that are photosynthetic,

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:55.680
but that are surprisingly adept at living
in low-light situations.

00:05:55.680 --> 00:06:02.280
And yet, they do still need light, which would
be fine, except that green sulfur bacteria

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:03.060
have a problem:

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:04.920
they cannot swim.

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:10.980
There are ways around this problem, like the
green sulfur bacteria that just go up in the

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:13.440
water column by producing gas vacuoles.

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:16.500
It’s like balloon travel in the microcosmos.

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:22.320
And much like actual balloon travel, it can
take a few days for the bacteria to get where

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:25.440
they want to go, so not always the optimal
choice.

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:30.180
But that is not a problem for&nbsp;
Chlorobium chlorochromatii&nbsp;

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:33.060
because they have found a different way to

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:34.500
get around the microcosmos.

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:40.440
A colorless, rod-shaped bacteria called
Candidatus Symbiobacter mobilis.

00:06:41.820 --> 00:06:46.860
Now we don’t have a good view of this organism
because in most of our videos, it’s surrounded

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:50.340
by the green sulfur bacteria that have latched
onto it.

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:55.500
But here you can get a glimpse of it in the
middle of a group of green bacteria assembling

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:57.120
a consortium around it.

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:01.260
The job of that central bacterium is to move.

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:08.220
Surrounded by anywhere from 20-70 of its green
sulfur bacteria companions, the bacterium

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:12.120
in the middle of it all takes the group to
light and to sulfide.

00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:17.100
And these consortium members might be in
communication with each other to find the light.

00:07:17.700 --> 00:07:24.840
But why would Candidatus Symbiobacter mobilis
agree to this relationship?

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:30.360
Well the most likely explanation is that they
get nutrients from their companions.

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:38.160
Now if that is truly the exchange being made,
then it’s a trade that has had a huge impact

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:39.780
on that central bacterium.

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:44.700
When scientists studied the genomes of both
Chlorobium chlorochromatii and Candidatus

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:49.020
Symbiobacter mobilis, they found that the
green sulfur bacteria didn’t seem to have

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:51.240
to change much to make the symbiosis happen.

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:56.640
The few genes they’d acquired that were
geared towards symbiosis helped make the cells

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:00.120
adhere to each other or changed the cell wall
a little bit.

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:07.740
In contrast, Candidatus Symbiobacter mobilis
seems to have lost a large number of its genes,

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:11.820
so much so that it cannot grow independently.

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:19.980
It needs the consortium to survive, and only
when the consortium divides can it divide as well.

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:25.680
When you say it like that, “the consortium”
sounds almost like a dystopian entity, one

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:30.960
that’s trapped the Candidatus Symbiobacter
mobilis into a life of shuttling green sulfur

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:34.740
bacteria around with no mind to its own needs.

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:40.320
But, like couldn’t that be said for any of
our own cells, they don't get to live their

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:41.880
own lives or divide at will.

00:08:41.880 --> 00:08:46.860
If they do, our immune system will kill them
because uncontrolled cell division is very

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:48.720
dangerous to the larger organism.

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:56.580
This consortium is, in a way, a wholly other
kind of organism, one that you likely have

00:08:56.580 --> 00:08:57.780
never even heard of.

00:08:57.780 --> 00:09:02.760
At its heart is a species of prokaryote
that cannot survive outside of it.

00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:11.040
The closer you look, the more you realize, life
does not let us draw hard lines around anything.

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:14.760
It simply does what works.

00:09:19.020 --> 00:09:25.020
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:30.060
And if you want to open your eyes to the bizarre
world that is just outside your door

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:34.200
and also in your own body,
don’t forget to check out microcosmos.store.

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:38.940
We just want to bring more people into this
amazing community.

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:42.240
So check it out, and maybe you can pick up
your own microscope,

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:47.100
or, if you just want to show your love for
microscopy and microscopic organisms, we

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:52.740
have things like hydra shirts, and tardigrade
mugs, and micro bumper stickers that you can

00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:57.660
pick up to let everyone around you know that
you are a tardigrade enthusiast.

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:04.920
The people on the screen right now, they are
